# {{ name }}

{{ description }}

An ordinary LangGraph tool-calling agent: one model node, one tool node and
the standard routing between them. The library supplies the governed model,
the governed tools, the scoped checkpointer and the per-request run arguments.

## Test it

```bash
uv run pytest agents/{{ name }}
```

The tests need no account and no network. Start a change with a test:

| To change | Start in |
| --- | --- |
| What a tool does | `tests/test_{{ package }}.py`, the tool tests at the top |
| How the agent behaves | `tests/test_{{ package }}.py`, with a scripted model |
| What the rules allow | `tests/test_{{ package }}_as_configured.py`, which runs the real rules file |
| The HTTP entry point | `tests/test_{{ package }}_service.py` |

## Run it

```bash
uv run {{ name }} "Say hello to Ada."
uv run {{ name }}-serve --port {{ port }}
curl -s localhost:{{ port }}/invoke -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"input": {"question": "Say hello to Ada."}, "thread_id": "demo"}'
```

{% if model_provider == "fake" %}
`.env` selects the `fake` model, which needs no account and echoes the
question. To use a real model, set `{{ var.model_provider }}` and
`{{ var.model_id }}` in `.env`.
{% else %}
`.env` selects the `{{ model_provider }}` model provider. Set
`{{ var.model_id }}` there before the first run.
{% endif %}

{% if servers %}
This agent calls the tools of {{ servers | join(" and ") }}. Start
{{ "them" if servers | length > 1 else "it" }} first, each in its own terminal,
or the agent stops with "could not be reached":

```bash
{% for server in servers %}
uv run {{ server }}
{% endfor %}
```

{% endif %}
On this machine a caller without a token is the development identity in
`{{ var.identity_options }}`. Local state is written under `.agentlib/`: the
audit log as JSON lines and the conversation checkpoints in SQLite.

## What to read

| File | Shows |
| --- | --- |
| `src/{{ package }}/tools.py` | The agent's own tools: plain functions |
| `src/{{ package }}/graph.py` | The graph, and the five places the library appears in it |
| `src/{{ package }}/__main__.py` | One question from the command line |
| `src/{{ package }}/service.py` | Serving the agent over HTTP |
| `.env.example` | How the service is configured locally |

## Adding a tool

1. Write a failing test for the function in `tests/test_{{ package }}.py`.
2. Write the function in `tools.py` and add it to the list in `graph.py`.
3. Name it in the rule `{{ name }}-calls-its-own-tools` in
   `policies/agentlib/rules/data.yaml`. Until then every call to it is denied,
   and `tests/test_{{ package }}_as_configured.py` is where you see that.
